Artefact 1B: Executable data-profiling specification (v2.0)

Handling rules

Runs in the secure layer. Owner fields are excluded.
Every count carries its denominator.
Secure output: exact counts, authorised analysts only.
Shared output: banded counts below a threshold set by the privacy policy owner (placeholder: 10).
No sample text leaves the secure layer.
Prior thresholds remain proposals.

Stages run in this order. P0 and P1 can change the design, so they run before everything else.

P0. Snapshot and key integrity
Maximum REPORTING_DATE per table, and whether the four tables share it. If not, report the mismatch and do not mix snapshots.
Within the selected snapshot, rows per business ID. Where duplicates remain, list the differentiating fields and apply no automatic selection.
Report raw row counts and deduplicated object counts.
Confirm that business IDs (ISS_ID, ACT_ID, INC_ID) are stable and are not surrogate keys.
P1. History feasibility (gate for all history-based tests)

For each of the four tables:

Number of reporting dates, frequency and gaps.
Distinct versions per object ID.
Whether changed values persist between snapshots.
Whether old snapshots hold full text or current values only.
Whether objects disappear after closure.
Conflicts between effective dates and reporting dates.
Whether snapshot history begins materially later than object history.

Classification output for each item below: A (directly available), B (potentially reconstructable), C (unavailable unless another table exists).

Action status events, revised due dates, cancellation timing
Issue rating history
Action set as it stood at closure
Scope changes
Independent-review history

Decision rule, agreed before the run: if history is not adequate, the MVP uses current values and explicit dates only. History-based tests are removed, not approximated. Reconstructed history is labelled “first observed at reporting date X” and kept separate from business-event history.

P2. Domain population rules (before any domain comparison)

For each candidate domain, define a versioned rule, then report:

The inclusion rule, with each rule family tried (folder hierarchy via ACT_FOLDER_LVL_*, process via INC_PRCSS, product, Cross Ref relationships, existing Payments population)
Object types included
Count per rule, and overlaps between rules
Orphaned objects, and objects assigned to several domains
Objects included only through narrative text
Precision on a reviewed sample of 50 included objects per object type

Payments goes through the same scorecard as the others.

P3. Population and eligibility
ID	Measure
P3.1	Closed issues by closure year and rating. Closure is defined by status if a status field exists, otherwise by ISS_CLSD_DT, and both rules are reported if both exist.
P3.2	ORGNL_ISU_RTNG against ISS_RTNG, and share downgraded
P3.3	Eligible counts per route R1 to R7 using placeholder thresholds, with overlaps. Source fields: ISS_SRC_CTGR_*, AUDIT_FNDNG, SOX_DEFCIENCY_TYPE, RELTED_TO_SOX.
P3.4	Closed in the last 90 days
P3.5	Existence of any structured independent-review marker, and its value distribution
P3.6	Incident impact value dictionaries and write-off distribution, for route R3 and R4 threshold setting
P4. Links (Cross Ref as principal source)
Counts by source and target object type and relationship type.
Duplicates, self-links, orphaned source and target IDs, directionality.
Active versus historical relationships, if a status field exists.
Many-to-many distributions.
Link stability across snapshots (subject to P1).
Reconciliation: Cross Ref issue-action links against ISSUE.ACTN_ID, and Cross Ref issue-incident links against ISS_INC_ASSOC_LIST. Report agreement, one-sided links and conflicts. Resolve the meaning of ACTN_ID and ACTN_COMPLTN_DT.
If Cross Ref quality supports it, designate it the principal source and keep the association fields as reconciliation sources.
P5. Narrative quality (task-specific)

Rater sample: 50 random eligible cases per domain, two raters. Each field is rated only against the tasks it feeds.

Tasks: weakness extraction, action-claim extraction, implementation-evidence review, verification-evidence review, incident retrieval. Each rating is Suitable or Not suitable.

P5.1 Per-field measures: population rate, length distribution, and rater-confirmed suitability per task. Fields: ISS_DESC, ISU_DTLS_OF_CTRL_GAP, ISS_ISU_CLOSER_SUMMARY, ACT_DESC, ACT_CLOSURE_SUMMARY, ACT_UPDATE_COMMENTARY, INC_DESC, INC_INCDNT_CAUSE, INC_CLSR_SUMMARY.

P5.2 Text defects (rate per field): placeholders (“N/A”, “refer attached”, “as discussed”), mostly IDs or URLs or system text, title duplicated in description, closure summary copied from action description, truncation, HTML or formatting artefacts, encoding problems, very long lifecycle commentary, contradictions within an object, embedded tables or lists, references to unavailable attachments.

P5.3 Repeated text: classify as identical full text, shared template with variable content, generic with little case-specific content, duplicate-record repetition, or legitimate standard statement. Measure residual case-specific content after removing common template language, which matters more than repetition frequency.

P5.4 Control-gap indicator reliability: share of records with IS_THR_CTRL_GAP set and ISU_DTLS_OF_CTRL_GAP populated, and the reverse.

P5.5 Leakage: for each field below, report whether it could reveal a target judgement or contain post-anchor content. Record when it was last modified relative to issue closure, where P1 allows.

ISS_ISU_CLOSER_SUMMARY and ACT_CLOSURE_SUMMARY (explicit verification statements)
ACT_UPDATE_COMMENTARY (written after later incidents)
ISU_DTLS_OF_CTRL_GAP (containing the expected remediation)
ACTNS_TO_PRVNT_REOCURNC
ISS_INC_ASSOC_LIST (retrospective links)

Two views are maintained. The review-current view is everything the reviewer can see today, and it supports the triage product. An as-at-closure view uses only information known by the relevant date, where reconstructable. Any later historical performance experiment must use the as-at view.

P6. Action sets and LLAC
Actions per eligible issue, with the distribution by band (0, 1, 2 to 3, 4+).
ACT_STATUS distribution, cancelled share, ACT_REASON_FOR_CANCELLATION populated rate, and what ACT_TRACKING contains.
ACT_CLOSURE_DT populated rate by status. Specifically, whether it is populated for cancelled actions.
LLAC state for every eligible issue:
LLAC available: at least one non-cancelled linked action with a valid completion date
No completed linked action
All linked actions cancelled
No linked action recorded (no-action cohort)
Action-link uncertainty: Cross Ref and association fields disagree materially
Generic action types (training, reminder, procedure update), identified by a rater-validated keyword screen.
Category C items (cancellation date, superseded link, required flag, dependency): state “unavailable” in the output and do not infer.
Complex or potential umbrella candidates (not exclusions). Signals: action count, description breadth, scope breadth, programme language, multiple processes or applications, duration, linked incident count. Mechanism count is added after labelling. Report the distribution of each signal and the overlap.
P7. Dates
Population and format consistency for every date column listed in 1A.
Order violations: completion before creation, closure before identification, closure before raising, dates in the future.
Issue closed before LLAC, and actions completed after issue closure.
Share with at least 6 months of post-LLAC observation before the extract date. Where LLAC is unavailable, report separately against the issue closure fallback, labelled as a fallback.
Issue closure is never reported as remediation completion.
P8. Incident pools and reporting indicators

For each eligible issue, report pool size under each of:

Exact process match
Descendant-process match
Application match
Exact process or application match
Domain-wide pool
Recorded issue-to-incident links

For each pool report: deduplicated occurrences (using INC_DUPLICATE_INCIDENT_ID), bulk incidents (INC_BULK_INC), missing occurrence date, missing usable narrative, and incidents outside continuous history. Report the candidate counts by temporal period.

Also report:

Share of issues with fewer than 20 or more than 2,000 candidates, by era and by reference type
At least 12 months of incident history before identification
Monthly incident counts per process, with gaps and step changes flagged (source continuity)
Reporting indicators per process-quarter: median lag from occurrence to INC_ENTRY_DT, share logged as No Impact or Low, NEAR_MISS share, volume. These test feasibility only.

The retrieval architecture is not finalised until this distribution is known.

P9. Entity resolution

For process and application references on issues (via Cross Ref), actions and incidents (INC_PRCSS, INC_OTHR_SYS), report each of these separately:

State	Measure
Populated	Field not empty
Identifier present	Stable ID rather than a name
Name only	
Exact match to master data	
Multiple possible matches	
Historic or retired entity	
Free text	
Unresolved	
Hierarchy node available	

If stable identifiers are absent, entity resolution is recorded as a separate risk and is not folded into retrieval.

P10. Taxonomy and eras
Value counts by year for ISS_ROOT_CAS_LVL_1/2, INC_ROOT_CAUSE_LVL_1/2, and the rating fields. Flag additions, retirements and relabelling.
Document request: change log for field definitions, closure requirements and source systems.
Proposed era boundaries, agreed before any sampling.
P11. Privacy

Rater-reviewed sample of 100 records across issue, action and incident text. Test for: customer names, employee names, email addresses, telephone numbers, account and reference numbers, addresses, free-text health or vulnerability information, complaint details, other sensitive customer information, legal or investigation material.

Report category, rate and false-removal behaviour of a candidate scrubber on non-person entities (applications, products, vendors, channels, locations, process names). Do not reproduce sample content. Policy on any finding sits with the privacy, data and model-risk functions.

P12. Token and payload volume
Character and word counts, estimated embedding and reasoning-model tokens, by object type and field
Cases above proposed payload limits
Duplicate text not to be reprocessed, and hash-unchanged records
Expected candidate-incident text volume per issue
A comparison of token volume against rater-assessed usefulness, to show which fields add evidence and which add only volume

Outputs inform chunking, whether an action set fits one call, whether lifecycle commentary is reduced, and whether retrieved incidents need staged summarisation.

P13. No-action cohort (separate branch)

Count by domain, era and rating. Source category, control-gap indicator, closure-summary usability. Share whose closure text explains why no action was required, share that cite an alternative remediation, and whether Cross Ref and the issue-level action fields agree that no action exists. This cohort is excluded from link accuracy and Tier 1 feasibility figures.

